--TEST--
`Core\Uri::parse` reports RFC 3986's components without normalizing one of them, and `Uri::tryParse` is the same parse with `null` where it throws — spec § 12, `rule:expressions/try-parse`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Uri::query
// covers: Core\Uri::scheme
// covers: Core\Uri::userInfo
// § 12: every component comes back exactly as written — still
// percent-encoded, still in the case it arrived in, with the default port
// still there. `parse` reports; it does not normalize.
var $u = Core\Uri::parse("https://alice@example.COM:8443/a/b%20c?x=1&y=2#top");
echo $u->toString(), "\n";
echo $u->scheme() as string, "\n";
echo $u->userInfo() as string, "\n";
echo $u->host() as string, "\n";
echo $u->port() as int, "\n";
echo $u->path(), "\n";
echo $u->query() as string, "\n";
echo $u->fragment() as string, "\n";

// § 12: `parse` takes a URI *reference*, which is what a request line
// carries, so a relative one is a `Uri` with three absent components.
var $r = Core\Uri::parse("/only/a/path");
echo $r->path(), "\n";
if ($r->scheme() == null) { echo "no scheme\n"; }
if ($r->userInfo() == null) { echo "no user info\n"; }
if ($r->port() == null) { echo "no port\n"; }
if ($r->fragment() == null) { echo "no fragment\n"; }

// § 12: an empty authority is not a missing one, which is the distinction
// `parse_url`'s array cannot hold.
var $f = Core\Uri::parse("file:///tmp/x");
echo "[", $f->host() as string, "]\n";
if ($r->host() == null) { echo "no host at all\n"; }

// § 12: `?` written with nothing after it is an empty query; no `?` is no
// query.
var $empty = Core\Uri::parse("http://h/p?");
if ($empty->query() == "") { echo "empty query\n"; }
var $none = Core\Uri::parse("http://h/p");
if ($none->query() == null) { echo "no query\n"; }

// § 12: an IPv6 literal is a host with its brackets on, since that is what
// RFC 3986 § 3.2.2's host component is.
var $six = Core\Uri::parse("http://[::1]:8080/x");
echo $six->host() as string, "\n";
echo $six->port() as int, "\n";

// `rule:expressions/try-parse`: `tryParse` is `parse` with `null` where it throws — and it
// is the *only* spelling of that question, `isValid` having been deleted so
// a validator can never drift from the parser. "Is this an absolute URI",
// the narrower question `isValid` used to ask, is that plus one reader call.
// `$s as ?Core\Uri` does not compile: § 3's class row has no exceptions.
?Core\Uri $absolute = Core\Uri::tryParse("https://example.com/a");
if ($absolute?->scheme() != null) { echo "absolute\n"; }
?Core\Uri $relative = Core\Uri::tryParse("/a/b");
if ($relative != null) {
    if ($relative->scheme() == null) { echo "a reference is not a URL\n"; }
}
?Core\Uri $spaced = Core\Uri::tryParse("http://example.com/a b");
if ($spaced == null) { echo "a space is not a URI byte\n"; }
--EXPECT--
https://alice@example.COM:8443/a/b%20c?x=1&y=2#top
https
alice
example.COM
8443
/a/b%20c
x=1&y=2
top
/only/a/path
no scheme
no user info
no port
no fragment
[]
no host at all
empty query
no query
[::1]
8080
absolute
a reference is not a URL
a space is not a URI byte
